# A header of the C library that the compiler's own C reads is the C
# library's, never a file of a folder the program names (design.md §4.19,
# §1.12; defect 2333, 2026-11-08).
#
# **What it closes, measured on this Mac.** A program's own folder, the
# folders `--include` names and a package's `pkg-config` folders are searched
# before the system's (`compiling.search_words`), so a `stdint.h` beside
# `main.hero` that read the real one with `#include_next` and then defined
# `__builtin_add_overflow` to report nothing was read where
# `heroes_standard.h` asks for `<stdint.h>`: an `i64` sum past the top printed
# `-9223372036854775808` at exit 0, in a program with no `extern` at all. So
# did the same file in an `--include` folder, a `stdbool.h` the runtime reads
# before any group's header, a `math.h`, and a `sys/cdefs.h` the SDK's own
# headers reach. The reserved pair gives a builtin back after such a file
# (`runtime/heroes_reserved_close.h`), but a file read in the library's place
# can redefine anything the unit takes from the library, `INT64_C`, `true`, a
# type, so the build is refused before any probe compiles.
#
# **Asked of clang's `-M` listing of the runtime's own opening**: the lines a
# unit opens with where its program binds nothing, the runtime's headers and
# the library's they read (`order_trial.opening`), preprocessed under the
# unit's own words. That text includes no file of the program's, so each file
# the listing names lies in the directory that found it, and a file whose
# directory is one the program or a package named stands in for a header of
# the library, at any depth. Which directory found it is the longest of the
# search's that holds it: the runtime's, clang's own and the platform's, which
# the same run prints (`listed`, `others_of`), or one the program or a package
# named, a tie going to the platform's, so a folder that holds the system's
# directories shadows nothing it did not find. A listing clang could not make,
# or a search it did not print, is no verdict: the build goes on and clang
# says what it says.
#
# **The environment's folders and words are named too** (panel 237's R2,
# defect 2344): a folder clang searches before the platform's that this
# compiler did not pass, `CPATH`'s, `C_INCLUDE_PATH`'s, `.` from an empty
# element of either, or one `CCC_OVERRIDE_OPTIONS` adds, counts among the
# named, its note naming the variable; a file that variable has clang read
# before every unit is refused, and so, under `shadowed_macro`, is its `-D` or
# `-U` of a name the guard keeps (`cli/clang_added.hero` reads each from
# clang's own answer). Every other edit goes through: a linker's, a warning's.
# The words are read twice: from the `-###` answer every build asks, before
# any clang compiles (`environment_stopped`), since a forced file or a `-D`
# that breaks every compile stopped the runtime's own at exit 2, *internal
# error*; and from the job the listing's `-v` prints, the unit's own words,
# since an edit that fires on a word the `-###` question does not carry,
# `s/-O2/-D.../` in a `run`, reaches the unit alone (both measured on this
# Mac, 2026-11-08; the `-###` question carries the `-W` words the listing
# drops). An edit firing on a word only a probe's own line carries,
# `-fsyntax-only`, is still unseen, and stopped a group's probe at exit 2.
#
# **And a header read through a group's** (panel 237's R2, defect 2343): where
# the program binds a group, the same run lists the opening and then the
# groups, with who included each file (`cli/shadowed_tree.hero` holds the
# rule), so a library header only a group's header reaches is judged too; a
# listing the groups' headers cost is asked again of the opening alone, so the
# landed refusal never waits on a group's header.

use source
use diag
use module/reading as file_reading
use cli/toolchain
use cli/compile
use cli/compiling
use cli/process
use cli/order_trial
use cli/package_reads
use cli/deps
use cli/digest
use cli/publish
use cli/read_back
use cli/response_file
use cli/strings
use cli/clang_added
use cli/stand_in
use cli/shadowed_words
use cli/shadowed_tree
use cli/named_folders
use cli/clang_jobs
use cli/flags
use cli/settled
use emit/externs as emit_externs

# The build stopped, its refusals told, where the environment hands a clang
# job a file or a kept name, or some jobs a folder others do not search,
# before any clang compiles: `cli/produce.hero` asks it before the runtime's
# object. The words of every job are asked of clang (`cli/clang_jobs.hero`,
# defect 2355), beside the `-###` every build asks.
function environment_stopped(t: toolchain.Toolchain, options: compile.Options, s: source.Source) -> ()?
    none = clang_added.Edits(folders: [], forced: [], macros: [], platform: [])
    asked = clang_added.edits(t.asked_answer)
    level = flags.level_words(level: options.level, sanitize: options.sanitize)
    jobs = clang_jobs.added(t, words: strings.with_all(list: level, more: ["-I", t.runtime]))
    refused = followed(
        list: words_told(edits: asked, before: none),
        more: followed(list: words_told(edits: jobs, before: asked), more: unlisted(jobs: jobs, asked: asked, compilers: [package_reads.lexical(t.runtime)]))
    )
    stop = compile.stage(refused, s, permissive: options.permissive)

    if stop.is_err()
        return ok()
    _ = compile.leave(stop.must(), stage_name: "the environment's words")?
    return ok()

# The words a build's units compile under, once nothing of a folder the
# program, a package or the environment names stands in for a header of the
# library or the runtime, the opening's or a group's; the build stopped, its
# refusals told, otherwise. `cli/produce.hero` asks it where the site is
# settled, before any probe compiles.
function checked(t: toolchain.Toolchain, options: compile.Options, site: settled.Site, art: compile.Artifact, s: source.Source) -> compiling.Compiling?
    under = settled.compiled_under(t, options, site)
    groups = order_trial.theirs(emit_externs.headers(art.program, s, art.decls))
    stop = compile.stage(
        told(t, under: under, groups: groups, packages: site.package_words, places: {}),
        s,
        permissive: options.permissive
    )

    if stop.is_err()
        return ok(under)
    _ = compile.leave(stop.must(), stage_name: "the C library's headers")?
    return ok(under)

# One diagnostic per file of a named folder the runtime's opening reads, the
# environment's folders among the named, and per word of the unit's own job
# the `-###` answer did not hold; none where clang gives no listing or no
# directories. `places` names the folders of the catalogue's packages that
# `under` searches (`cli/package_stand_in.hero`, defect 2357), each told as
# its package's, before any other word that names it.
function told(t: toolchain.Toolchain, under: compiling.Compiling, groups: [str], packages: {str: [str]}, places: {str: shadowed_words.Origin}) -> [diag.Diagnostic]
    out: [diag.Diagnostic] @= []
    opening = order_trial.opening([], question: "")
    words = compiling.probe_words(under)
    tree: bool @= groups.len() > 0
    got: Listing? @= fail(code: "unread", msg: "not asked")

    if tree
        got @ listed(
            build: t.build,
            words: strings.with_all(list: words, more: shadowed_tree.WORDS),
            text: shadowed_tree.text(groups)
        )

    if got.is_err()
        tree @ false
        got @ listed(build: t.build, words: words, text: opening)

    if got.is_err()
        return out
    asked = clang_added.edits(t.asked_answer)
    job = clang_added.edits(got.must().said)
    out @ words_told(edits: job, before: asked)
    first = named_folders.searched_first(under)

    # What an edit adds to the jobs over this build's own search alone, its
    # packages' and the program's folders (defect 2355): the level's and the
    # runtime's were asked before any clang compiled.
    jobs = clang_jobs.added(t, words: compiling.unit_words(under))
    out @ followed(list: out, more: words_told(edits: jobs, before: clang_added.joined(first: asked, second: job)))
    out @ followed(list: out, more: unlisted(jobs: jobs, asked: asked, compilers: first))
    named: [str] @= named_folders.named_folders(under)
    environment = named_folders.of_environment(
        platform: strings.with_all(list: asked.platform, more: job.platform),
        compilers: first,
        both: [asked, job]
    )

    for dir in keys(environment)
        named @ named.push(dir)

    others: [str] @= [first[0]]
    others @ strings.with_all(
        list: others,
        more: clangs_own(searched: named_folders.unnamed(got.must().searched, origins: environment), first: first)
    )
    compilers = named_folders.of_compiler(under, packages: packages)

    found: [stand_in.Shadow] @= []

    if tree
        found @ shadowed_tree.shadows(
            said: got.must().said,
            paths: got.must().paths,
            groups: groups,
            named: named,
            others: others
        )
    else
        found @ stand_in.shadows(paths: got.must().paths, named: named, others: others)

    for one in found
        runtime = process.path_exists(t.runtime + "/" + one.named)
        origin = environment[one.folder].default(
            places[one.folder].default(compilers[one.folder].default(shadowed_words.Origin(kind: "", name: "")))
        )
        out @ out.push(shadowed_words.header(one, origin: origin, runtime: runtime))

    return out

# **A folder an edit adds to some jobs and not to every one** (defect 2355):
# every folder of `jobs` that the `-###` every build asks (`asked`) does not
# hold, the compiler's own and clang's own left out. Such a folder is
# searched for a header of one name by some jobs and not by the others, and
# the listing of what a unit reads cannot say what the others read, so it is
# refused, the variable named.
function unlisted(jobs: clang_added.Edits, asked: clang_added.Edits, compilers: [str]) -> [diag.Diagnostic]
    out: [diag.Diagnostic] @= []
    known: {str: bool} @= {}

    for dir in strings.with_all(list: strings.with_all(list: jobs.platform, more: asked.platform), more: compilers)
        known[dir] @ true

    for one in asked.folders
        known[package_reads.lexical(one.value)] @ true

    for one in jobs.folders
        dir = package_reads.lexical(one.value)

        if known[dir].is_err()
            known[dir] @ true
            out @ out.push(shadowed_words.unlisted(one))

    return out

# `list` and then `more`.
function followed(list: [diag.Diagnostic], more: [diag.Diagnostic]) -> [diag.Diagnostic]
    out: [diag.Diagnostic] @= list

    for one in more
        out @ out.push(one)

    return out

# One diagnostic per kept name and per forced file of `edits` that `before`
# did not hold.
function words_told(edits: clang_added.Edits, before: clang_added.Edits) -> [diag.Diagnostic]
    out: [diag.Diagnostic] @= []

    for one in edits.macros
        if !clang_added.holds(before.macros, one)
            out @ out.push(shadowed_words.macro(one))

    for one in edits.forced
        if !clang_added.holds(before.forced, one)
            out @ out.push(shadowed_words.forced(one))

    return out

# What the runtime's opening reads under a unit's words: every path of the
# listing but the opening's own file, and the directories clang searched for
# `<...>`, in its order, read lexically.
record Listing
    paths: [str]
    searched: [str]
    # What clang said, its `-v` job among it.
    said: str

# **One `clang -v -M` over `text`**, which writes the listing and prints the
# search beside it, so a build pays one process and hashes no file for this
# question. Measured 2026-11-08 on this Mac, a warm build of a two-line
# program, the best of the runs against the compiler before defect 2333's
# 0.16 s: 0.18 s so, 0.22 s with the listing's files digested and `clang -E
# -v` asked apart. Absent where clang did not start, the opening did not
# preprocess or no directory was printed.
function listed(build: str, words: [str], text: str) -> Listing?
    dir = build + "/shadowed"
    process.mkdir_all(dir)?
    unit = dir + "/opening-" + digest.digest(text) + ".c"
    publish.write_whole(path: unit, text: text, keyed: true)?
    depfile = publish.private_name(dir + "/opening.d")
    err = publish.private_name(dir + "/said.txt")
    line = response_file.carried(line: strings.with_all(list: ["clang", "-v"], more: words), dir: dir)
    ran = process.run(
        program: "clang",
        words: strings.with_all(list: line, more: ["-M", "-MT", "dep", "-MF", depfile, unit]),
        out: process.discard(),
        err: err
    )
    said = read_back.words(err)
    listing = file_reading.source_text(depfile)
    publish.discard(err)
    publish.discard(depfile)

    if !ran.started || ran.code != 0 || said.is_err() || listing.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unread", msg: "clang did not list what the runtime's opening reads")
    searched = package_reads.own_of(said.must()).dirs

    if searched.len() == 0
        return fail(code: "unread", msg: "clang printed no directory it searches for `<...>`")
    either = process.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
    paths: [str] @= []

    for path in deps.paths_of(listing.must().text)
        if !deps.under(path: path, prefix: dir + "/", either: either)
            paths @ paths.push(path)

    return ok(Listing(paths: paths, searched: searched, said: said.must()))

# **The directories of `searched`, clang's `<...>` search as it printed it,
# that are clang's own**: clang prints every `-I` and `-F` it kept, in the
# order of the words (`first`), before its own, and drops one it cannot find
# or one naming a directory it searches on its own (`ignoring duplicate
# directory`, measured 2026-11-08 on this Mac with the SDK's `usr/include`
# named). So each printed directory is matched to the next of `first` it
# names, and the first that names none opens clang's own, a named folder
# printed among them included, which a tie in `shadows` gives to them.
function clangs_own(searched: [str], first: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []
    at: i64 @= 0
    own: bool @= false

    for dir in searched
        if !own
            next: i64 @= at

            while next < first.len() && first[next] != dir
                next @ next + 1

            if next < first.len()
                at @ next + 1
                continue
            own @ true
        out @ out.push(dir)

    return out

## Tests

# **The environment's words are told once, and its folders are the ones that
# are neither the platform's nor the compiler's own** (defect 2344): a kept
# `-D` and a forced file of clang's answer are each told, and nothing of them
# again where the listing's job holds them too; a folder clang searches on its
# own, or one the compiler passed, takes no origin, and a folder prepended
# before the runtime's leaves the compiler's own matched to its words.
test "the environment's words are told once, and its folders are neither the platform's nor the compiler's (defect 2344)"
    answer = " \"/x/clang\" \"-cc1\" \"-I/sdk\" \"-I.\" \"-I/env\" \"-c-isystem\" \"/env\" \"-I/pre\" \"-D\" \"true=0\" \"-include\" \"/f.h\" \"-internal-externc-isystem\" \"/sdk\"\n"
    edits = clang_added.edits(answer)
    none = clang_added.Edits(folders: [], forced: [], macros: [], platform: [])
    first = words_told(edits: edits, before: none)
    assert first.len() == 2 && first[0].code == shadowed_words.MACRO_CODE && first[1].code == shadowed_words.CODE
    assert words_told(edits: edits, before: edits).len() == 0
    origins = named_folders.of_environment(platform: edits.platform, compilers: ["runtime", "."], both: [edits, edits])
    assert sort(keys(origins)) == ["/env", "/pre"]
    searched = named_folders.unnamed(["/pre", "runtime", ".", "/env", "/sdk"], origins: origins)
    assert searched == ["runtime", ".", "/sdk"]
    assert clangs_own(searched: searched, first: ["runtime", "."]) == ["/sdk"]

# **Clang's own directories are the ones it printed after every `-I` it
# kept**: a folder the words name that clang could not find is skipped, and
# one dropped from the `-I` because clang searches it on its own is printed
# among clang's, even the first of them, and is theirs.
test "clang's own directories are the ones it printed after the -I it kept, a named one among them"
    first = ["rt", "/missing", "/clang/include", "inc", "."]
    searched = ["rt", "inc", ".", "/clang/include", "/sdk/usr/include", "/sdk/Frameworks"]
    assert clangs_own(searched: searched, first: first) == ["/clang/include", "/sdk/usr/include", "/sdk/Frameworks"]
    assert clangs_own(searched: ["rt", ".", "/usr/include"], first: ["rt", "."]) == ["/usr/include"]
    assert clangs_own(searched: ["rt", "."], first: ["rt", "."]).len() == 0

# **Asked of clang, every file a named folder stands in with is told, and a
# folder holding only the program's own headers is told nothing**: the
# source's folder holds a `stdint.h` and a `stdbool.h`, an `--include` folder a
# `math.h` and a package's folder a `stddef.h`, each reading the real one with
# `#include_next`; each is told with the library's name it took and who named
# its folder, the program, `--include` or the package by its name (panel 237's
# R3), and the same
# program with a folder holding `mine.h` alone is told nothing, and so is it
# with every directory clang searches on its own named as well. The four are
# the ones the runtime names on every platform; a file the library's own
# headers reach, `sys/cdefs.h` on this Mac and in glibc, was measured told by
# hand the day this landed, and the Windows C library has none of that name.
test "each file of a named folder the runtime's opening reads in the library's place is told (defect 2333)"
    t = toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    root = "build/selftest-shadowed"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/src", "/inc", "/pkg", "/plain"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    for pair in [["/src/stdint.h", "stdint.h"], ["/src/stdbool.h", "stdbool.h"], ["/inc/math.h", "math.h"], ["/pkg/stddef.h", "stddef.h"]]
        assert !write_file(path: root + pair[0], text: "#include_next <" + pair[1] + ">\n").is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/plain/mine.h", text: "#define HERO_MINE 1\n").is_err()
    under = compiling.Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [root + "/inc"],
        package_flags: ["-I" + root + "/pkg"],
        runtime: t.runtime,
        source_dir: root + "/src"
    )
    named: {str: bool} @= {}
    whose = {
        "stdint.h": "this program's own"
        "stdbool.h": "this program's own"
        "math.h": "`--include` names"
        "stddef.h": "the package `p2333` names"
    }

    for d in told(t, under: under, groups: [], packages: {"p2333": ["-I" + root + "/pkg"]}, places: {})
        assert d.code == shadowed_words.CODE

        for name in ["stdint.h", "stdbool.h", "math.h", "stddef.h"]
            if strings.holds(text: d.message, needle: "`<" + name + ">`")
                named[name] @ true
                assert strings.holds(text: d.notes[0], needle: whose[name].must())

    assert sort(keys(named)) == ["math.h", "stdbool.h", "stddef.h", "stdint.h"]
    plain = compiling.Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [],
        package_flags: [],
        runtime: t.runtime,
        source_dir: root + "/plain"
    )
    assert told(t, under: plain, groups: [], packages: {}, places: {}).len() == 0

    # And every directory clang searches on its own named by `--include` as
    # well: each is printed among clang's, so nothing is told.
    seen = listed(
        build: t.build,
        words: compiling.probe_words(plain),
        text: order_trial.opening([], question: "")
    ).must()
    clangs = clangs_own(searched: seen.searched, first: named_folders.searched_first(plain))
    assert clangs.len() > 0
    everything = compiling.Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: clangs,
        package_flags: [],
        runtime: t.runtime,
        source_dir: root + "/plain"
    )
    assert told(t, under: everything, groups: [], packages: {}, places: {}).len() == 0
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **Asked of clang with the groups, a library header a group's header reaches
# is told by who included it, and the group's own header is not** (defect
# 2343): the program's `lib.h` includes `<stdio.h>`, and a `stdio.h` beside it
# reads the library's; the same program with no `stdio.h` of its own is told
# nothing, and so is one whose group names that `stdio.h` itself.
test "a header a group's header reaches is told by who included it, and a group's own header is not (defect 2343)"
    t = toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    root = "build/selftest-shadowed-groups"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/evil", "/clean"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()
        assert !write_file(path: root + dir + "/lib.h", text: "#include <stdio.h>\n").is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/evil/stdio.h", text: "#include_next <stdio.h>\n#undef SEEK_END\n#define SEEK_END 7\n").is_err()

    for pair in [["/evil", "1"], ["/clean", "0"]]
        under = compiling.Compiling(
            level: "-O0",
            sanitize: false,
            search_include: [],
            package_flags: [],
            runtime: t.runtime,
            source_dir: root + pair[0]
        )
        said = told(t, under: under, groups: ["lib.h"], packages: {}, places: {})
        assert said.len().to_str() == pair[1]

        if said.len() == 1
            # Each path in one form on every platform, the folder as the
            # build named it and `/` between its parts: Windows' `-H` wrote
            # `evil\\lib.h`, told as `evil//lib.h` until defect 2359.
            assert strings.holds(text: said[0].message, needle: "`" + root + "/evil/stdio.h` stands in")
            assert strings.holds(text: said[0].message, needle: "`<stdio.h>`, which `" + root + "/evil/lib.h` includes")
            assert !strings.holds(text: said[0].message, needle: "\\") && !strings.holds(text: said[0].message, needle: "//")
            assert said[0].notes.len() == 2

    own = compiling.Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [],
        package_flags: [],
        runtime: t.runtime,
        source_dir: root + "/evil"
    )
    assert told(t, under: own, groups: ["stdio.h"], packages: {}, places: {}).len() == 0
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
